description:
Establishes the workspace-wide contract for how JMAP method handlers and backends learn who the authenticated caller is. The current settled design — 'Auth: Not in dispatcher, caller's responsibility before dispatch' — leaves an empty contract on the `CallerCtx` associated type. JMAP method semantics that depend on caller identity (chat role hierarchy, mail $seen on shared mailboxes, calendar ACLs, sharing/myRights, metadata isPrivate visibility) cannot be honored against an opaque `CallerCtx`.

This epic locks in the foundation answer that is intended to outlive the workspace.

## Principles

1. **Identity is a foundation concept, not an extension feature.** Decide once in jmap-server; every extension inherits.
2. **`CallerCtx` is the seam: foundation declares the contract, deployment decides the content.** Associated type stays; contract extends.
3. **Backend is canonical.** Handlers may add defense-in-depth pre-checks for fast-fail but the backend is the single source of truth for permission enforcement, atomic with the mutation.

## The shape

```rust
pub trait JmapBackend: Send + Sync + 'static {
    type CallerCtx: Clone + Send + Sync + 'static;

    // ... existing methods ...

    /// The caller's stable identity within this account namespace.
    ///
    /// Returns `None` for deployments that have not wired identity
    /// (test fixtures, single-user dev servers). A `None`-returning
    /// backend CANNOT honor JMAP semantics that depend on caller
    /// identity — chat role-hierarchy, calendar ACLs, sharing/myRights,
    /// per-user $seen on shared mailboxes, etc. Authentication is
    /// still the HTTP layer's job; this method exposes the result of
    /// that authentication to the JMAP layer for in-method semantics.
    ///
    /// Implementations MUST NOT mint identity — they MUST read it
    /// from the `CallerCtx` populated by the HTTP/auth middleware
    /// before `dispatch()` was called.
    fn principal_id(caller: &Self::CallerCtx) -> Option<&jmap_types::Id> {
        let _ = caller;
        None
    }
}
```

## Locked-in rules (workspace policy)

- The ONLY way a JMAP method or backend asks 'who is the caller' is `JmapBackend::principal_id`.
- Handlers do NO permission checking. Single source of truth = backend.
- If a future extension needs more than an id (groups, claims, roles), it adds that to its OWN backend trait — NOT to JmapBackend. Foundation gives you the id; extensions own the meaning.
- No 'caller_identity returns generic blob' escape hatch. Typed return (`&Id`).
- Federation does NOT need a separate hook here — federation handler maps peer-signed identity to a local principal once, before invoking the JMAP method. The JMAP method sees a normal `principal_id()`.

## Decomposition

  .1 — Add `principal_id` method on `JmapBackend` with default impl + tests.
  .2 — Update workspace AGENTS.md and crate-jmap-server/AGENTS.md to document the seam + 'backend canonical' rule.

## Downstream consumers

This epic unblocks all identity-dependent semantics across the workspace. First consumer is JMAP-g7wu.2.4 (chat Space/set permission enforcement). Future consumers: jmap-mail-server (RFC 8621 $seen on shared mailboxes), jmap-calendars-server (calendar ACLs), jmap-sharing-server (RFC 9670 myRights), jmap-metadata-server (isPrivate visibility scoping).

## Status

Architectural decision approved by Mark, 2026-05-11. Children to be picked up by the autonomous /do-beads loop.
